# Lesson 3, new exercise: the lesson's K64 opposite A875 diamond layout. Eight sure tricks; the ninth comes from the long diamond.
title: The fourth diamond
id: L03-09
type: exercise
lesson: 3
concepts: 3.2
student: N
board: 41
dealer: N
vulnerable: EW

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  AQ4    AQJ    A875   962
  E  JT98   T97    QJ3    AKT
  S  K73    K82    K64    8543
  W  652    6543   T92    QJ7

auction:
  N 1NT*   = 15-17 points, balanced
     not *: 17 points and a balanced hand: what does the lesson open?
  E Pass
  S 2NT    = 8-9 points, balanced: invites game
  W Pass
  N 3NT*   = Accepts: a maximum 1NT
     key: Partner invites with 8-9. You have 17, the top of your range, so accept.
     not Pass: Partner invited you to bid on with the top of your range. 17 is the top. Did you add 17 and 8?
     not *: Partner's 2NT asks: are you at the top of your 15-17?
  E Pass
  S Pass
  W Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1NT, and partner's 2NT invited game. With 17, the top of your
  range, you accepted. You are declarer, because you bid no-trump first, and
  so East will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  winners: 8
  spades: 3 = The ace, king and queen.
  hearts: 3 = The ace, king and queen.
  diamonds: 2 = Two on top. A third only if the opponents' six split 3-3: worth trying, but well short of an even chance, so don't count it.
  clubs: 0 = None.
  remind: You need 9 tricks. Count the obvious winners in each suit.
  remind: Look at the diamonds: dummy's K64 and your A875. The opponents have six. What if they break 3-3?
play:
  1   E SJ  S S3  W S2  N SQ
      note: Eight winners on top: three spades, three hearts and two diamonds. The ninth can come from your fourth diamond, if the opponents' six diamonds split three and three.
  2   N D5  E D3  S D4  W D9
      note: Start on diamonds, and duck the first round: play low from both hands. You have to lose a diamond anyway, so lose it now, while you still control the other suits.
  3   W CQ  N C2  E CA  S C3
      note: The defense takes its club tricks.
  4   E CK  S C4  W C7  N C6
  5   E CT  S C5  W CJ  N C9
  6   W H3  N HJ  E H7  S HK
      note: Dummy's king goes on your jack, so the hearts don't get stuck: the high card from the short side.
  7   S H8  W H4  N HQ  E H9
      note: Back to your hearts.
  8   N HA  E HT  S H2  W H5
      note: Your last heart winner.
  9   N D7  E DJ  S DK  W D2
      note: Now the diamonds again. Both opponents followed to the first round.
  10  S D6  W DT  N DA  E DQ
      note: Back to your ♦A. Both opponents followed three times, so your last diamond is now a winner.
  11  N D8  E S8  S C8  W H6
      note: The fourth diamond is your ninth trick.
  12  N S4  E S9  S SK  W S5
      note: Now the spades, over to dummy's king.
  13  S S7  W S6  N SA  E ST
      note: 3NT made.

play recap:
  The plan: count eight winners on top and look for the ninth in the long
  diamonds.

  You had to lose a diamond whatever happened, so you lost it on the first
  round. That kept the ace and king to take control later, and it gave the
  defense less to work with. The diamonds split 3-3, which happens only
  about a third of the time, so you needed a little luck.

  Lose the tricks you have to lose early in the play.
